  "review": { "authority": "first_party_published", "method": "dual_reviewer", "reviewers": [{ "id": "spec-registry-rebuild-lane-20260819", "reviewedOn": "2026-08-19" }, { "id": "normalization-review-20260829", "reviewedOn": "2026-08-29" }], "notes": "Normalized from the 2026-08-19 rebuild research. The 64 MB per-file figure is published on the form and is recorded as allowed rather than required: it is the identical binary round value on all three slots with no rationale beside it, which reads as the hosting platform's ceiling rather than a photographic ask, and a talent should not be told JAG wants 64 MB files. The three shots come from the example captions rather than from the slot labels, so they are derived rather than explicit, and the unknown records that the form never says which example belongs to which slot. No format is asserted: the file inputs carry no accept attribute at all, and the extension allowlist lives on the platform's server where it was not observable. Two facts are recorded here rather than in the rules: the form's LinkedIn control is the platform's anti-spam honeypot, described in its own markup as being for validation purposes and to be left unchanged - it must never be presented to a talent as a field to fill - and the page's safety notice states that every JAG employee's email address ends in @jagmodels.com, which is the agency's own anti-impersonation claim and belongs to the verification layer rather than to a submission rule. The site publishes no eligibility of any kind, and an earlier internal premise that JAG is positioned for women of all sizes was falsified: that phrase appears nowhere on the site, the published positioning is broader, and the form has no gender field." }
